abstract: "This paper constructs the first chronology of banking crises in Hong Kong between 1865 and 2010. Using this chronology, it then examines their macroeconomic indicators and consequences. Combining a newly compiled bank stock price index with historical newspapers and the existing literature, I identify 11 banking crises over 160 years, including several previously unrecorded episodes involving Chinese native banks in the 1920s and 1930s. Nineteenth-century crises originated in foreign exchange banks tied to the London money market, whereas later crises increasingly centred on Chinese banks. Using vector autoregressions, I find that exchange rate depreciation, deflation, and high interest rates signal crises in the pre-war period, while slow GDP growth is the key indicator after 1945. I also find that the estimated impact of crises on the wider economy is limited. One likely explanation is that non-bank institutions may have provided alternative funding when formal banks came under stress."
